.TH P3M-CP 1 "@DATE@" "p3m @VERSION@" "p3m Manual"
.SH NAME
p3m-cp \- parallel copy
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B p3m-cp
.RI [ OPTIONS ]
.IR SOURCE ...
.I DEST
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B p3m-cp
copies files and directory trees in parallel, recursively, like
.BR "cp \-R" .
If
.I DEST
is an existing directory the sources are copied into it; otherwise a single
.I SOURCE
is copied to the path
.IR DEST .
.PP
By default this is a
.B dry run
that lists everything that would be copied; add
.B \-\-apply
to copy.
Existing destination entries are skipped and reported unless
.B \-\-overwrite
is given.
File data is moved with
.BR copy_file_range (2)
(reflink or server-side copy where available), with a read/write fallback.
.PP
Directory modes, ownership and timestamps are applied deepest-first after the
walk, so read-only trees copy correctly.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Actually copy (the default is a dry run).
.TP
.B \-\-overwrite
Replace existing destination entries; without it they are skipped and
reported.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-preserve
Preserve mode, ownership (when permitted) and timestamps.
The default is mode masked by the umask, as with
.BR cp .
.TP
.BR \-j ", " \-\-threads " \fIN\fR"
Worker threads (default: the number of online CPUs).
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIFILE\fR"
Write the CSV (\fIsource,type,size,destination,result\fR) to
.I FILE
and show a live progress display.
.TP
.BR \-q ", " \-\-quiet
Suppress the console listing; progress and the summary are still shown, and a
.B \-o
file is still written.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Show usage and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Show the version and exit.
.SH SAFETY
Symbolic links are never followed; they are recreated as links.
Copying a directory into itself is refused before any work starts.
Destination files are created with
.B O_EXCL
so data is never written through a symlink planted at the destination.
.SH SIGNALS
On the first
.B SIGINT
or
.B SIGTERM
during
.BR \-\-apply ,
.B p3m-cp
prints a notice, stops picking up new directories and lets every in-flight
file copy finish, so no destination file is left truncated.
Directories already populated still receive their final metadata.
The summary reports
.B interrupted
and the exit status is 130 (SIGINT) or 143 (SIGTERM).
A second signal terminates immediately, possibly leaving one partially
written file.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success (skipped existing destinations are not errors).
.TP
.B 1
Completed with errors.
.TP
.B 2
Usage error, invalid target, or into-itself refusal.
.TP
.BR 130 ", " 143
Interrupted by SIGINT or SIGTERM.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
p3m\-cp /data/project /backup
p3m\-cp \-\-apply \-p /data/project /backup
p3m\-cp \-\-apply \-\-overwrite \-j 16 /data/a /data/b /mnt/nfs/dest
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR p3m (7),
.BR p3m\-mv (1),
.BR p3m\-diff (1),
.BR cp (1)
